From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Apr 19 19:30:00 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 19 Apr 2016 19:30:00 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] change of variables for compound parameter
Message-ID: <4gO1agqYEvs.4dqXWCk6BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <4gO1agqYEvs.HJKydEAjBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <4gO1agqYEvs.HJKydEAjBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/4gO1agqYEvs/m/4dqXWCk6BQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The density beta(2,2) is only defined for outcomes in (0, 1).
So you'd need to ensure log(A) / B in (0, 1).  Then you
run into the usual multiplicative identifiability issue, here
with a log in the middle:

  log(A) / B === log(A^N) / (B/N)

If N = -1, you get sign flipping and multimodality.

This also means your transform isn't invertible (the inverse is
one to many), and thus there's no way to calculate the Jacobian.
At least I'm pretty sure that's what happens --- someone else should
check my work here, because I still count Jacobians on my fingers
and toes.

- Bob

> On Apr 19, 2016, at 8:30 AM, Daniel Rosenbloom <daniel.r...@gmail.com> wrote:
