Hi Daniel,

Thanks for the response.  I used the zip from here <https://github.com/stan-dev/cmdstan/releases/download/v2.9.0/cmdstan-2.9.0.zip>, but I think I found my problem -- among the warnings and errors was a "FIND: Parameter format not correct" (I've been basically pasting every line of the output into google one at a time, looking for clues), and I found this thread <https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/ubCvAcw0lwQ> in which someone had the Rtools utilities on their path, but the windows find.exe from Windows\system32 was earlier (this is normal as the system path is typically pre-pended to the user path as far as I can tell).  My solution was to remove the Rtools directories from my user path and add them to the system path, but earlier than the Windows\system32 entry (in my case, by right-clicking the windows menu > System > Advanced System Settings > Environment Variables, and then editing the user and system variables.  I think if you don't have admin rights, you might be out of luck though, since the system path's Windows\system32\find.exe will always supersede  the user's path's Rtools\bin\find.exe.  This might be worth a mention in Appendix B.3 in the cmdstan guide.

Thanks,
Dan

