Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have two vectors parameters: g[J] and s[J], where g[j]~beta(1,1) and s[j]~beta(1,1), with j=1,2,..,J.
Could someone help me put the following restriction:
0 <= g[j] + s[j] < 1, for all j=1,2,...,J

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sat Apr 30 21:22:02 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sat, 30 Apr 2016 21:22:02 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Parameters constraint
Message-ID: <r-LxO3By7dw.8FAkvKigCAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <r-LxO3By7dw.g8PuyxWdCAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <r-LxO3By7dw.g8PuyxWdCAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/r-LxO3By7dw/m/8FAkvKigCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Your constraint is convenient because you're really
asking for the first two components of the cumulative
distribution over the simplex.

data {
  int<lower=0> J;
}
parameters {
  simplex[3] theta[J];
}
transformed parameters {
  vector[J] g;
  vector[J] s;

  g <- to_vector(theta[, 1]);
  s <- g + to_vector(theta[, 2]);
}

Because beta(1, 1) is uniform, those priors on s and j won't
do anything --- Stan only needs the log density up to a constant.
So you can just drop them.

The resulting prior is uniform over the (g,s) pairs meeting
the constraint (the points in (0, 1) x (0, 1) above the diagonal).

- Bob

